 "stop": "3 quatrains",
 "poem": "There are, but on the top of a station,\n'Twere only worth while one's own aid betray\nTo watch their exits and come to salvation.\nFor whom? - The quiet middle-crested gay\nThat in the fields of London sit away,\nAnd without their changing stations see\nHuge plumes and pinnacles of service sway,\nAnd glassy avenues where the busy\nAnd proud wenders grow slow as with resignation.\nBut whose cloying speech would such pride elevate,\nOr his low virile air degrade an\nExcuseless man? For I contemplate",
